Sort the array and let `dp[i]` be the number of trees whose root is `arr[i]`. Start each `dp[i]` at 1 (the single-node tree). Then for every `j < i` with `arr[i] % arr[j] == 0`, let `k = arr[i] / arr[j]`. If `k` is in the array, add `dp[j] * dp[k]` to `dp[i]`. The answer is the sum of all `dp[i]`, modulo 10^9+7.

Products of two dp values can reach about 10^18, which exceeds 2^53, so plain `number` multiplication would lose precision. I use `BigInt` for the multiplication. With n = 1000 there are about 500k pairs, which is fine.

A map from value to index lets me look up `k` quickly. Sorting ascending guarantees that `dp[j]` and `dp[k]` are already computed, since both `arr[j]` and `k` are less than `arr[i]` (all values are at least 2).

```typescript
function numFactoredBinaryTrees(arr: number[]): number {
    const MOD = 1000000007n;
    const a = [...arr].sort((x, y) => x - y);
    const n = a.length;
    const idx = new Map<number, number>();
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) idx.set(a[i], i);
    const dp: bigint[] = new Array(n).fill(1n);
    let total = 0n;
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        let cur = 1n;
        for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
            if (a[i] % a[j] === 0) {
                const k = a[i] / a[j];
                const ki = idx.get(k);
                if (ki !== undefined) {
                    cur = (cur + dp[j] * dp[ki]) % MOD;
                }
            }
        }
        dp[i] = cur;
        total = (total + cur) % MOD;
    }
    return Number(total);
};
```

