The examples show strings with a trailing space (e.g. `"dcce "`), which is probably an artifact of how the problem was formatted. The real inputs are lowercase letters only. To be safe, `f` ignores any character that is not a lowercase letter, so a trailing space is never picked as the smallest character.

Approach:
1. Compute `f(s)` for each word. Since word length is at most 10, `f` is at most 10.
2. Build a count array `cnt[v]` of words with `f = v`, then a suffix sum so `suffix[v]` is the number of words with `f >= v`.
3. For each query, the answer is `suffix[f(q) + 1]`, the number of words with `f(W) > f(q)`.

Implementation details:
- `f` scans for the smallest character code between `a` and `z`, counting its frequency.
- Size the array at 13 so that index `f(q) + 1` (at most 11) is always in range.

```typescript
function numSmallerByFrequency(queries: string[], words: string[]): number[] {
    const f = (s: string): number => {
        let min = 200;
        let c = 0;
        for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
            const code = s.charCodeAt(i);
            if (code < 97 || code > 122) continue;
            if (code < min) {
                min = code;
                c = 1;
            } else if (code === min) {
                c++;
            }
        }
        return c;
    };
    const suffix = new Array(13).fill(0);
    for (const w of words) {
        suffix[f(w)]++;
    }
    for (let v = 11; v >= 0; v--) {
        suffix[v] += suffix[v + 1];
    }
    return queries.map(q => suffix[Math.min(f(q) + 1, 12)]);
}
```

