The maximum OR is the OR of all elements, since OR never loses bits. With at most 16 elements, a DFS over include/exclude choices visits at most 2^16 subsets, which is fast enough. A subset counts when its OR equals the maximum. The empty subset has OR 0, which is below the maximum because every `nums[i]` is at least 1, so it is never counted.

The starter snippet is a function rather than a class, so I keep the function signature exactly as given.

```typescript
function countMaxOrSubsets(nums: number[]): number {
    let target = 0;
    for (const x of nums) target |= x;
    let count = 0;
    const n = nums.length;
    const dfs = (i: number, cur: number): void => {
        if (i === n) {
            if (cur === target) count++;
            return;
        }
        dfs(i + 1, cur | nums[i]);
        dfs(i + 1, cur);
    };
    dfs(0, 0);
    return count;
};
```

