For each start index `i`, extend `j` to the right while `word[j]` is a vowel. Keep a bitmask of the vowels seen so far. Whenever the mask equals 31 (all five vowels present), count that substring. If a non-vowel appears, stop extending from this `i`.

This is O(n²), which is fine for n ≤ 100.

The examples in the statement have trailing spaces, e.g. "aeiouu ". The real constraints say the word is lowercase letters only, so the space is just a formatting artifact. Even if a space were present, it is not a vowel and would break the run, so the code handles it correctly either way.

```typescript
function countVowelSubstrings(word: string): number {
    const idx: { [k: string]: number } = { a: 1, e: 2, i: 4, o: 8, u: 16 };
    let count = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < word.length; i++) {
        let mask = 0;
        for (let j = i; j < word.length; j++) {
            const b = idx[word[j]];
            if (!b) break;
            mask |= b;
            if (mask === 31) count++;
        }
    }
    return count;
};
```

