Build a map from each key to its value, then scan `s` once. Characters outside brackets are copied as they are. On `(`, find the matching `)`, look up the key between them, and append its value, or `?` if the key is unknown. Collecting pieces in an array and joining at the end keeps the work linear in the length of `s`.

The examples show trailing spaces inside the strings, which look like formatting artifacts. Since `s` is only lowercase letters and brackets, I treat the strings as given and do no special handling for spaces.

```typescript
function evaluate(s: string, knowledge: string[][]): string {
    const map = new Map<string, string>();
    for (const [k, v] of knowledge) map.set(k, v);
    const res: string[] = [];
    let i = 0;
    const n = s.length;
    while (i < n) {
        if (s[i] === '(') {
            const j = s.indexOf(')', i);
            const key = s.substring(i + 1, j);
            const v = map.get(key);
            res.push(v === undefined ? '?' : v);
            i = j + 1;
        } else {
            res.push(s[i]);
            i++;
        }
    }
    return res.join('');
};
```

