The approach is a reverse-graph topological sort (Kahn's algorithm), which avoids recursion depth problems with n up to 10^4.

1. For each node, set its out-degree to `graph[i].length`.
2. Build the reverse graph: for every edge i→j, add i to `rev[j]`.
3. Start a queue with all nodes whose out-degree is 0 (the terminal nodes).
4. Pop a node and mark it safe. For each predecessor in `rev[node]`, decrement its out-degree. If that reaches 0, push it onto the queue.
5. Collect the safe nodes in ascending index order.

The starter shape is a function, not a class, so I keep it exactly as given.

```typescript
function eventualSafeNodes(graph: number[][]): number[] {
    const n = graph.length;
    const outdeg: number[] = new Array(n).fill(0);
    const rev: number[][] = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        outdeg[i] = graph[i].length;
        for (const j of graph[i]) {
            rev[j].push(i);
        }
    }
    const queue: number[] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (outdeg[i] === 0) queue.push(i);
    }
    const safe: boolean[] = new Array(n).fill(false);
    let head = 0;
    while (head < queue.length) {
        const node = queue[head++];
        safe[node] = true;
        for (const p of rev[node]) {
            outdeg[p]--;
            if (outdeg[p] === 0) queue.push(p);
        }
    }
    const res: number[] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (safe[i]) res.push(i);
    }
    return res;
};
```

