Approach: scan the array once, keeping `cnt[x]` (occurrences of each value), `freq[c]` (how many values occur exactly `c` times), and `maxF` (the largest count so far). After adding the element at index `i`, the prefix has length `len = i + 1`. The prefix is valid if one of these holds:

1. `maxF == 1`: every value appears once, so removing any one element works.
2. `freq[maxF] * maxF + 1 == len` and `freq[1] == 1`: exactly one value occurs once and every other value occurs `maxF` times. This covers the case where the single-occurrence value is removed, including when all other values share a count.
3. `freq[maxF] == 1` and `freq[maxF-1] * (maxF-1) + maxF == len`: one value occurs `maxF` times and every other value occurs `maxF-1` times. Removing one occurrence of the top value equalizes them. When `maxF-1 == 0`, case 1 already covers it.
4. Only one distinct value is present, so `freq[maxF] * maxF == len` with `freq[maxF] == 1`. This is covered by case 3 with `freq[maxF-1] = 0`, since then `maxF == len`.

For case 2, if `freq[maxF] * maxF + 1 == len` and `freq[1] == 1` with `maxF > 1`, the single-occurrence value is the only one not counted in `freq[maxF] * maxF`, so the condition is consistent. If `maxF == 1`, case 1 already applies.

