The examples show trailing spaces inside the strings (e.g. `"hello world "` and `"ad "`). To be safe, I handle them like this:

- Build a set of broken letters, ignoring any character that isn't a lowercase letter (so a trailing space in `brokenLetters` is skipped).
- Split `text` on spaces and skip empty tokens, so a trailing space doesn't produce an extra word.
- Count each word that contains no broken letter.

The starter shape is a function rather than a class, so I keep that signature.

```typescript
function canBeTypedWords(text: string, brokenLetters: string): number {
    const broken = new Set<string>();
    for (const c of brokenLetters) {
        if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') broken.add(c);
    }
    let count = 0;
    for (const word of text.split(' ')) {
        if (word.length === 0) continue;
        let ok = true;
        for (const c of word) {
            if (broken.has(c)) {
                ok = false;
                break;
            }
        }
        if (ok) count++;
    }
    return count;
};
```

