Binary search on the running time T. A time T is feasible if the sum of min(b, T) over all batteries is at least n*T.

The sum can reach 1e5 * 1e9 = 1e14, and n*T can reach about the same size. Both are below 2^53 ≈ 9e15, so plain numbers are exact.

The upper bound on T is sum/n, which is at most 1e14. I search lo=1 to hi=floor(sum/n). Each check is O(m) and the search takes about 47 iterations, which is fine.

Binary search: lo=0, hi=floor(sum/n). While lo<hi, set mid = floor((lo+hi+1)/2). If feasible(mid), set lo=mid, otherwise hi=mid-1. Computing mid as Math.floor((lo+hi+1)/2) stays exact because lo+hi is at most 2e14.

The function signature is kept as given.

```typescript
function maxRunTime(n: number, batteries: number[]): number {
    let sum = 0;
    for (const b of batteries) sum += b;
    let lo = 0;
    let hi = Math.floor(sum / n);
    while (lo < hi) {
        const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi + 1) / 2);
        let total = 0;
        for (const b of batteries) {
            total += b < mid ? b : mid;
        }
        if (total >= n * mid) lo = mid;
        else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return lo;
};
```

