This is a bitmask DP over rows. For each row, I compute `avail`, a mask of the good seats. A row mask `mask` is valid if `mask & ~avail` is 0 and `mask & (mask >> 1)` is 0, meaning no two students sit side by side.

Between consecutive rows, a student at column `j` in the current row must not have a student at `j-1` or `j+1` in the previous row. So `mask & (prev << 1)` and `mask & (prev >> 1)` must both be 0.

`dp[mask]` is the best total ending with `mask` in the current row, with -1 marking unreachable states. The initial previous state is `dp[0] = 0`.

Input characters may be `'.'` or `'#'`. The examples show trailing spaces, but the real input is just those characters, so I treat `'.'` as a good seat and anything else as broken. To be safe against stray spaces, I check `seats[i][j].trim() === '.'`.

The function signature is kept as given.

```typescript
function maxStudents(seats: string[][]): number {
    const m = seats.length;
    const n = seats[0].length;
    const size = 1 << n;
    const bits = (x: number): number => {
        let c = 0;
        while (x) {
            c += x & 1;
            x >>= 1;
        }
        return c;
    };
    let dp: number[] = new Array(size).fill(-1);
    dp[0] = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
        let avail = 0;
        for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            if (seats[i][j].trim() === '.') avail |= 1 << j;
        }
        const nd: number[] = new Array(size).fill(-1);
        for (let mask = 0; mask < size; mask++) {
            if (mask & ~avail) continue;
            if (mask & (mask >> 1)) continue;
            const c = bits(mask);
            for (let prev = 0; prev < size; prev++) {
                if (dp[prev] < 0) continue;
                if (mask & (prev << 1)) continue;
                if (mask & (prev >> 1)) continue;
                const v = dp[prev] + c;
                if (v > nd[mask]) nd[mask] = v;
            }
        }
        dp = nd;
    }
    let best = 0;
    for (const v of dp) if (v > best) best = v;
    return best;
};
```

