Since n ≤ 300, brute force is fast enough: for each candidate node in `initial`, remove it, simulate the spread, and count the infected nodes.

For each candidate r:
1. Mark r as removed and ignore it entirely.
2. Run a BFS starting from every other initial node, skipping r.
3. Count the visited nodes.

Each BFS costs O(n²) on the adjacency matrix, and there are up to n candidates, so the total is O(n³) = 27M operations, which is fine.

Choose the candidate with the smallest count, breaking ties by the smaller index. Iterating over the candidates in sorted order and updating only on a strictly smaller count handles the tie-break.

```typescript
function minMalwareSpread(graph: number[][], initial: number[]): number {
    const n = graph.length;
    const sorted = [...initial].sort((a, b) => a - b);
    let best = -1;
    let bestCount = Infinity;
    for (const r of sorted) {
        const visited = new Array<boolean>(n).fill(false);
        visited[r] = true; // removed
        const queue: number[] = [];
        for (const s of initial) {
            if (s === r) continue;
            visited[s] = true;
            queue.push(s);
        }
        let count = queue.length;
        let head = 0;
        while (head < queue.length) {
            const u = queue[head++];
            const row = graph[u];
            for (let v = 0; v < n; v++) {
                if (row[v] === 1 && !visited[v]) {
                    visited[v] = true;
                    count++;
                    queue.push(v);
                }
            }
        }
        if (count < bestCount) {
            bestCount = count;
            best = r;
        }
    }
    return best;
}
```

