I use an iterative BFS, which avoids recursion-depth problems on skewed trees with up to 10^5 nodes. The first leaf reached in level order is the nearest one, so its depth is the answer.

An empty tree returns 0. The queue is an array with a head index, so dequeuing is O(1).

The starter snippet is a function rather than a class, so I keep that shape.

```typescript
function minDepth(root: TreeNode | null): number {
    if (!root) return 0;
    const queue: TreeNode[] = [root];
    const depths: number[] = [1];
    let head = 0;
    while (head < queue.length) {
        const node = queue[head];
        const d = depths[head];
        head++;
        if (!node.left && !node.right) return d;
        if (node.left) {
            queue.push(node.left);
            depths.push(d + 1);
        }
        if (node.right) {
            queue.push(node.right);
            depths.push(d + 1);
        }
    }
    return 0;
};
```

