This is a binary search on the number of days, since if m bouquets can be made by day d, they can also be made on any later day.

1. If m * k > n, there are not enough flowers, so return -1. The product is at most 1e6 * 1e5 = 1e11, which is safe in a JavaScript number.
2. Search the range from the minimum to the maximum value in bloomDay.
3. For a candidate day d, scan the array while counting a run of consecutive flowers with bloomDay <= d. When the run reaches k, count one bouquet and reset the run. When a flower has not bloomed, reset the run. The day is feasible if the bouquet count reaches m.
4. Take the smallest feasible day.

The starter shape is a function declaration, so I keep it exactly as given.

```typescript
function minDays(bloomDay: number[], m: number, k: number): number {
    const n = bloomDay.length;
    if (m * k > n) return -1;
    let lo = Infinity, hi = 0;
    for (const b of bloomDay) {
        if (b < lo) lo = b;
        if (b > hi) hi = b;
    }
    const can = (d: number): boolean => {
        let count = 0, run = 0;
        for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (bloomDay[i] <= d) {
                run++;
                if (run === k) {
                    count++;
                    run = 0;
                    if (count >= m) return true;
