This is a bitmask DP over subsets of tasks.

Let `dp[mask]` be the pair (sessions, time used in the last session), minimized lexicographically. Start with `dp[0] = (1, 0)`. For each mask and each task `i` not in it, compute the new pair:

- If `time + tasks[i] <= sessionTime`, the task fits in the current session, giving `(sessions, time + tasks[i])`.
- Otherwise a new session is opened, giving `(sessions + 1, tasks[i])`.

Keep the lexicographically smaller pair for `mask | (1 << i)`. The answer is `dp[full].sessions`.

Encoding the pair as a single number `sessions * 100 + time` makes the comparison a plain numeric `<`. Since `time <= 15`, this is safe. Initialize `dp[0] = 100` (1 session, 0 time) and every other entry to `Infinity`.

For a transition from value `v`, let `s = Math.floor(v / 100)` and `t = v % 100`. The new value is `s*100 + t + tasks[i]` if `t + tasks[i] <= sessionTime`, otherwise `(s+1)*100 + tasks[i]`.

With n = 14 this is about 16384 × 14 transitions, which is trivial.

```typescript
function minSessions(tasks: number[], sessionTime: number): number {
    const n = tasks.length;
    const full = (1 << n) - 1;
    const dp = new Array<number>(full + 1).fill(Infinity);
    dp[0] = 100;
    for (let mask = 0; mask < full; mask++) {
        const v = dp[mask];
        if (v === Infinity) continue;
        const s = Math.floor(v / 100);
        const t = v % 100;
        for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (mask & (1 << i)) continue;
            let nv: number;
            if (t + tasks[i] <= sessionTime) {
                nv = s * 100 + t + tasks[i];
            } else {
                nv = (s + 1) * 100 + tasks[i];
            }
            const nm = mask | (1 << i);
            if (nv < dp[nm]) dp[nm] = nv;
        }
    }
    return Math.floor(dp[full] / 100);
};
