The approach is a monotonic stack over the array traversed twice, to handle the circular wrap-around.

1. Initialize the result array with -1 for every position.
2. Loop `i` from 0 to 2n-1 and use `nums[i % n]` as the current value.
3. While the stack is non-empty and the value at the index on top of the stack is less than the current value, pop that index and set its result to the current value.
4. During the first pass only (`i < n`), push `i` onto the stack.

Each index is pushed once and popped at most once, so the time is O(n) and the extra space is O(n).

```typescript
function nextGreaterElements(nums: number[]): number[] {
    const n = nums.length;
    const res: number[] = new Array(n).fill(-1);
    const stack: number[] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) {
        const v = nums[i % n];
        while (stack.length && nums[stack[stack.length - 1]] < v) {
            res[stack.pop()!] = v;
        }
        if (i < n) stack.push(i);
    }
    return res;
};
```

