The count comes from a recursive formula. For an array, the root is the first element. Split the rest into `left` (elements smaller than the root) and `right` (elements larger), keeping their original order. The number of orderings is

C(|left|+|right|, |left|) · ways(left) · ways(right),

and the final answer is this count minus 1, since the original ordering is excluded.

Binomial coefficients come from Pascal's triangle mod p, a 1001×1001 table. Each entry stays below about 1e9+7, and a sum of two such entries is safe in doubles.

Multiplying two values near 1e9 exceeds 2^53, so I use BigInt for the multiplications. The recursion depth is at most 1000, which is fine. Each level costs O(n), so the total is O(n²) in the worst case.

The function is `numOfWays` with the starter signature, and the recursion lives in a nested helper `f`.

Pascal's triangle is built per call, which is 1M entries. To save memory I use an array of Int32Array rows; values below 1e9+7 fit in int32, since 1e9+7 < 2^31.

```typescript
function numOfWays(nums: number[]): number {
    const MOD = 1000000007;
    const MODn = BigInt(MOD);
    const n = nums.length;
    const C: Int32Array[] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
        const row = new Int32Array(i + 1);
        row[0] = 1;
        row[i] = 1;
        for (let j = 1; j < i; j++) {
            row[j] = (C[i - 1][j - 1] + C[i - 1][j]) % MOD;
        }
        C.push(row);
    }
    function f(arr: number[]): bigint {
        if (arr.length <= 2) return 1n;
        const root = arr[0];
        const left: number[] = [];
        const right: number[] = [];
        for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
            if (arr[i] < root) left.push(arr[i]);
            else right.push(arr[i]);
        }
        let res = BigInt(C[left.length + right.length][left.length]);
        res = (res * f(left)) % MODn;
        res = (res * f(right)) % MODn;
        return res;
    }
    const total = f(nums);
    return Number((total - 1n + MODn) % MODn);
};
```

