Approach: bitmask DP over subsets of `nums`.

1. Compute the total sum. If it isn't divisible by `k`, return false. Let `target = sum / k`.
2. If any element exceeds `target`, return false.
3. Let `dp[mask]` be the sum of the elements in `mask` modulo `target`, or -1 if `mask` is unreachable.
4. Set `dp[0] = 0`. For each reachable `mask` and each unused element `i`, if `dp[mask] + nums[i] <= target`, set `dp[mask | 1<<i] = (dp[mask] + nums[i]) % target`.
5. The answer is whether `dp[full] == 0`, where `full = (1<<n) - 1`.

Why this works: filling a subset one element at a time never lets the current partial group exceed `target`. A mask is reachable only if its elements can be ordered so that the groups are filled exactly in sequence. When the sum reaches `target`, the remainder resets to 0 and a new group starts. Since the total is `k * target`, reaching the full mask means exactly `k` groups were formed.

Each element is positive, so every group is non-empty. The sum of the full mask is `k * target`, and `dp[full]` is 0 exactly when the full mask is reachable, because unreachable masks stay at -1.

With n ≤ 16 this is 65536 × 16 operations, which is fast.

```typescript
function canPartitionKSubsets(nums: number[], k: number): boolean {
    const n = nums.length;
    let sum = 0;
    for (const x of nums) sum += x;
    if (sum % k !== 0) return false;
    const target = sum / k;
    for (const x of nums) if (x > target) return false;
    const full = (1 << n) - 1;
    const dp = new Int32Array(1 << n).fill(-1);
    dp[0] = 0;
    for (let mask = 0; mask < full; mask++) {
        if (dp[mask] < 0) continue;
        for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (mask & (1 << i)) continue;
            const s = dp[mask] + nums[i];
            if (s <= target) {
                const nm = mask | (1 << i);
                if (dp[nm] < 0) dp[nm] = s % target;
            }
        }
    }
    return dp[full] === 0;
};
```

