I'll use a union-find (DSU). For each request, I find the roots of u and v. If the roots are the same, the two are already connected, so the request succeeds. Otherwise I check every restriction (x, y): if the roots of x and y are exactly the roots of u and v (in either order), merging would connect a restricted pair, so the request fails. If no restriction blocks it, I union u and v and the request succeeds.

This costs O(requests × restrictions × α) ≈ 10^6 operations, which is fast enough.

The starter signature is a standalone function rather than a class, so I'm keeping it exactly as given.

```typescript
function friendRequests(n: number, restrictions: number[][], requests: number[][]): boolean[] {
    const p: number[] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) p.push(i);
    const find = (x: number): number => {
        while (p[x] !== x) {
            p[x] = p[p[x]];
            x = p[x];
        }
        return x;
    };
    const res: boolean[] = [];
    for (const [u, v] of requests) {
        const a = find(u), b = find(v);
        if (a === b) {
            res.push(true);
            continue;
        }
        let ok = true;
        for (const [x, y] of restrictions) {
            const rx = find(x), ry = find(y);
            if ((rx === a && ry === b) || (rx === b && ry === a)) {
                ok = false;
                break;
            }
        }
        if (ok) p[a] = b;
        res.push(ok);
    }
    return res;
};
```

