This is a standard DP problem. Let `dp[i][j]` be true when `s[i..]` matches `p[j..]`, and fill the table from the end of both strings.

- Base case: `dp[m][n] = true`, since two empty suffixes match.
- For each `i` from `m` down to 0 and `j` from `n-1` down to 0, check whether the first characters match: `first = i < m && (p[j] === s[i] || p[j] === '.')`.
- If `p[j+1] === '*'`, either skip the starred element (`dp[i][j+2]`) or consume one character and stay on the same pattern position (`first && dp[i+1][j]`).
- Otherwise, `dp[i][j] = first && dp[i+1][j+1]`.

The starter snippet is a function rather than a class, so I keep the function signature exactly as given. The table has size (m+1) × (n+2) so that `j+2` never goes out of bounds.

```typescript
function isMatch(s: string, p: string): boolean {
    const m = s.length, n = p.length;
    const dp: boolean[][] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i <= m; i++) {
        dp.push(new Array(n + 2).fill(false));
    }
    dp[m][n] = true;
    for (let i = m; i >= 0; i--) {
        for (let j = n - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
            const first = i < m && (p[j] === s[i] || p[j] === '.');
            if (j + 1 < n && p[j + 1] === '*') {
                dp[i][j] = dp[i][j + 2] || (first && dp[i + 1][j]);
            } else {
                dp[i][j] = first && dp[i + 1][j + 1];
            }
        }
    }
    return dp[0][0];
};
```

