2. Run a DFS over the characters, tracking the current index, the number of open parentheses in the built string, the remaining left and right removals, and the string built so far.
3. For a `(` or `)` there are two choices. Skip it if the matching removal budget is above zero, or keep it. Keeping a `)` is only allowed when the open count is above zero.
4. Letters are always kept.
5. When the index reaches the end and both removal budgets are zero, the string is valid, so add it to a set.
6. To avoid duplicate work, skip consecutive identical parentheses. Choosing which of a run of identical parentheses to remove gives the same result, so only the duplicates that come from the set matter. Using a `Set` for the results handles correctness, and the input is small (at most 20 parentheses).
