The array is a path, so I build an adjacency map and walk it from one end.

1. Build a `Map<number, number[]>` from each value to its neighbours.
2. The two endpoints of the path are the only values with exactly one neighbour. Pick either as the start.
3. Walk the path: from the current value, move to the neighbour that isn't the previous value. Since all elements are unique, this never revisits a value. Stop once `n = adjacentPairs.length + 1` elements are placed.

The required signature is a function `restoreArray`, so I keep that shape.

```typescript
function restoreArray(adjacentPairs: number[][]): number[] {
    const adj = new Map<number, number[]>();
    for (const [u, v] of adjacentPairs) {
        if (!adj.has(u)) adj.set(u, []);
        if (!adj.has(v)) adj.set(v, []);
        adj.get(u)!.push(v);
        adj.get(v)!.push(u);
    }
    let start = 0;
    for (const [k, v] of adj) {
        if (v.length === 1) {
            start = k;
            break;
        }
    }
    const n = adjacentPairs.length + 1;
    const res: number[] = [start];
    let prev = NaN;
    let cur = start;
    while (res.length < n) {
        const nb = adj.get(cur)!;
        let next = nb[0];
        if (next === prev) next = nb[1];
        res.push(next);
        prev = cur;
        cur = next;
    }
    return res;
};
```

