This is a bitmask DP over which letters of `target` are already covered.

Let `dp[mask]` be the minimum number of stickers needed to cover the letters in `mask`, with `dp[0] = 0`. For each reachable mask, find the first unset bit `i`. Some sticker must cover that letter, so only stickers containing `target[i]` are tried. Applying a sticker greedily fills every uncovered target position whose letter is still available in the sticker.

Details:
1. Precompute the letter counts of each sticker.
2. For a given mask and sticker, copy the counts. Then for each position j not in the mask, if `cnt[target[j]] > 0`, decrement it and set bit j in the new mask.
3. Update `dp[newmask] = min(dp[newmask], dp[mask] + 1)`.
4. The answer is `dp[full]`, or -1 if it is unreachable.

Always branching on the first unset bit keeps the search small. The state space is 2^15 masks × 50 stickers × about 15 positions, roughly 25M operations, which is fine.

The signature must be a function, as in the starter code. The examples show trailing spaces in the words, but the real constraints say lowercase letters only, so no special handling is needed. I only index counts for characters `a`–`z`.

```typescript
function minStickers(stickers: string[], target: string): number {
    const n = target.length;
    const full = (1 << n) - 1;
    const counts: Int32Array[] = stickers.map(s => {
        const c = new Int32Array(26);
        for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
            const k = s.charCodeAt(i) - 97;
            if (k >= 0 && k < 26) c[k]++;
        }
        return c;
    });
    const t: number[] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) t.push(target.charCodeAt(i) - 97);
    const INF = 1e9;
    const dp = new Int32Array(full + 1).fill(INF);
    dp[0] = 0;
    const tmp = new Int32Array(26);
    for (let mask = 0; mask < full; mask++) {
        if (dp[mask] >= INF) continue;
        let first = 0;
        while ((mask >> first) & 1) first++;
        const need = t[first];
        for (const c of counts) {
            if (c[need] === 0) continue;
            tmp.set(c);
            let nm = mask;
            for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if ((nm >> j) & 1) continue;
                if (tmp[t[j]] > 0) {
                    tmp[t[j]]--;
                    nm |= 1 << j;
                }
            }
            if (dp[mask] + 1 < dp[nm]) dp[nm] = dp[mask] + 1;
        }
    }
    return dp[full] >= INF ? -1 : dp[full];
}
```

