Approach: build a trie of the reversed words. Keep the stream as an array of character codes, and on each query walk the trie from the newest character backwards. Return true as soon as a node marks the end of a word, and stop when no child exists. The walk is bounded by the longest word (200 characters), so each query costs at most 200 steps.

The examples show words with trailing spaces (e.g. "cd "), which looks like a formatting artifact. The real words are lowercase letters only, so I'll use 26 children per node. To be safe against stray characters, I'll map each character with `charCodeAt - 97` and skip any code outside 0–25 while building the trie. A skipped character in a word would be harmless in practice, since the real input has no spaces.

Implementation:
- The trie is stored in flat arrays: `children` is an `Int32Array` of size nodes×26 (up to 2000×200+1 = 400001 nodes, so about 10.4M entries, roughly 41MB). To use less memory, I'll use a growing regular array of per-node `Int32Array(26)` created lazily.
- The stream is stored as a number array. Only the last 200 characters matter, so I'll keep the whole array, since there are at most 40000 queries.
- For the query, start at the root and iterate i from the end of the stream down to max(0, length − maxLen). At each step, move to the child. If it is missing, return false. If it is terminal, return true.

```typescript
class StreamChecker {
    private nodes: Int32Array[] = [];
    private end: boolean[] = [];
    private stream: number[] = [];
    private maxLen = 0;

    constructor(words: string[]) {
        this.nodes.push(new Int32Array(26));
        this.end.push(false);
        for (const w of words) {
            let cur = 0;
            let len = 0;
            for (let i = w.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
                const c = w.charCodeAt(i) - 97;
                if (c < 0 || c > 25) continue;
                len++;
                let nx = this.nodes[cur][c];
                if (nx === 0) {
                    nx = this.nodes.length;
                    this.nodes.push(new Int32Array(26));
                    this.end.push(false);
                    this.nodes[cur][c] = nx;
                }
                cur = nx;
            }
            if (len > 0) this.end[cur] = true;
            if (len > this.maxLen) this.maxLen = len;
        }
    }

