1. Compute the in-degree of every node. If any node has in-degree greater than 1, return false.
2. Exactly one node must have in-degree 0 (the root). If there are zero or several, return false.
3. Run a DFS or BFS from the root, tracking visited nodes. If a node is reached twice, return false (this is already ruled out by the in-degree check, but it is cheap to include). At the end, the visited count must equal n, which catches cycles that are disconnected from the root.

Example 3: both nodes have in-degree 1, so there is no root and the answer is false.

Example 2: node 3 has in-degree 2, so the answer is false.

The traversal uses an iterative stack to avoid deep recursion.

```typescript
function validateBinaryTreeNodes(n: number, leftChild: number[], rightChild: number[]): boolean {
    const indeg = new Array(n).fill(0);
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (leftChild[i] !== -1) {
            indeg[leftChild[i]]++;
        }
        if (rightChild[i] !== -1) {
            indeg[rightChild[i]]++;
        }
    }
    let root = -1;
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (indeg[i] > 1) return false;
        if (indeg[i] === 0) {
            if (root !== -1) return false;
            root = i;
        }
    }
    if (root === -1) return false;
    const visited = new Array(n).fill(false);
    const stack: number[] = [root];
    visited[root] = true;
    let count = 0;
    while (stack.length) {
        const u = stack.pop()!;
        count++;
        for (const c of [leftChild[u], rightChild[u]]) {
            if (c !== -1) {
                if (visited[c]) return false;
                visited[c] = true;
                stack.push(c);
            }
        }
    }
    return count === n;
};
```

