This uses the greedy two-pointer approach with backtracking to the most recent `*`.

1. Walk through `s` with index `i` and through `p` with index `j`. Keep `star`, the position of the last `*` seen in `p` (initially -1), and `mark`, the position in `s` where that `*` started matching.
2. If `p[j]` is `?` or equals `s[i]`, advance both pointers.
3. If `p[j]` is `*`, record `star = j` and `mark = i`, then advance `j` only. This first tries to match the `*` against the empty sequence.
4. On a mismatch, if there is a previous `*`, backtrack: set `j = star + 1`, increment `mark`, and set `i = mark`. This makes the `*` absorb one more character of `s`.
5. If there is a mismatch and no previous `*`, return false.
6. After `s` is consumed, skip any trailing `*` in `p`. The answer is true only if `j` has reached the end of `p`.

The worst case is O(n·m) time with O(1) extra space.

The starter shape is a function rather than a class, so I keep that signature as given.

```typescript
function isMatch(s: string, p: string): boolean {
    let i = 0, j = 0, star = -1, mark = 0;
    const n = s.length, m = p.length;
    while (i < n) {
        if (j < m && (p[j] === '?' || p[j] === s[i])) {
            i++;
            j++;
        } else if (j < m && p[j] === '*') {
            star = j;
            mark = i;
            j++;
        } else if (star !== -1) {
            j = star + 1;
            mark++;
            i = mark;
        } else {
            return false;
        }
    }
    while (j < m && p[j] === '*') j++;
    return j === m;
};
```

