This is a shortest-path problem on an unweighted graph, so I use BFS. The starter snippet is a plain function rather than a class, so I keep that signature.

1. Put `wordList` in a set. If `endWord` is not in it, return 0.
2. Start BFS from `beginWord` with a level count of 1.
3. For each word taken from the queue, try every position and every letter `a`–`z`. If the new word is in the set, it is an unvisited neighbor.
4. If a neighbor equals `endWord`, return the current level plus 1.
5. Otherwise, delete the neighbor from the set so it is never visited twice, and add it to the queue.
6. If the queue empties without reaching `endWord`, return 0.

Cost is about 5000 words × 10 positions × 26 letters, with string building at each step. That is fast enough.

I use an index pointer into the array instead of `shift()` to keep dequeuing O(1), and I process the queue level by level.

```typescript
function ladderLength(beginWord: string, endWord: string, wordList: string[]): number {
    const set = new Set<string>(wordList);
    if (!set.has(endWord)) return 0;
    let queue: string[] = [beginWord];
    let level = 1;
    set.delete(beginWord);
    while (queue.length > 0) {
        const next: string[] = [];
        for (const w of queue) {
            for (let i = 0; i < w.length; i++) {
                const pre = w.slice(0, i);
                const post = w.slice(i + 1);
                for (let c = 97; c < 123; c++) {
                    const ch = String.fromCharCode(c);
                    if (ch === w[i]) continue;
                    const nw = pre + ch + post;
                    if (set.has(nw)) {
                        if (nw === endWord) return level + 1;
                        set.delete(nw);
                        next.push(nw);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        queue = next;
        level++;
    }
    return 0;
};
```

